  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Suppose you conduct a sequence of independent trials with two possible outcomes: success or failure. For each trial success occurs with probability $p$, and failure occurs with probability $1-p$, where $0<p\\leq1$. What is the expected number of trials needed to achieve your first success, including the trial on which that success occurs?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{p}$ trials.\n\nUse the probabilities $P(X=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p$ and the weighted sum of the possible waiting times.",
      "worked": "Let $X$ count the trials up to and including the first success. For $k=1,2,3,\\ldots$,\n$$P(X=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p.$$\nUsing $1+2r+3r^2+\\cdots=\\dfrac{1}{(1-r)^2}$ for $|r|<1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}E(X)&=1p+2(1-p)p+3(1-p)^2p+\\cdots\\\\E(X)&=p\\left(1+2(1-p)+3(1-p)^2+\\cdots\\right)\\\\E(X)&=\\frac{p}{\\left(1-(1-p)\\right)^2}\\\\E(X)&=\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe expected number is $\\dfrac{1}{p}$ trials, including the successful trial.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
